 The decimal expansion of \( \frac{1}{1111} \) is a repeating decimal with a period of 4. We can determine this by performing the division. \[
\frac{1}{1111} = 0.\overline{0009}
\] The repeating block is "0009". Therefore, the first 40 digits after the decimal point will repeat this block 10 times. The sum of the digits in one repeating block is \(0 + 0 + 0 + 9 = 9\). Since this block repeats 10 times in the first 40 digits, the sum of the first 40 digits is \(9 \times 10 = 90\). </think> <answer> 90 </answer>

